  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#browser-interface-elements\n\n> To allow Web pages to integrate with Web browsers, certain Web browser interface elements are exposed in a limited way to scripts in Web pages.\n\nSo, this is the closest you have to a rationale for the BarProp objects, but it gives basically no information about how the extremely limited functionality is useful, or why treating unimplemented UI elements as absent is forbidden for everything except the toolbar and the statusbar.  The lack of any guidance as to what these are for, combined with the requirement to treat unimplemented UI elements as visible, seems to suggest that this stuff is only meant for compatibility with existing scripts; what it _doesn't_ do is explain what decisions those scripts are making based on this \"visibility information\" that motivates acting as if unimplemented UI elements (which the UA's developers can't find an analog for) are available, or what's so special about the toolbar and statusbar.\n\n(Also, it's _slightly_ odd that `Window.status` is explicitly specified to do **nothing** except retain the last string assigned to it, but `Window.statusbar` is not similarly stubbed out.)\n",
